1
00:00:00,230 --> 00:00:04,400
Welcome to the first of four short
videos on how to present your project.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:09,440
This one is about the most important
skill of all, telling it as a story.

3
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,440
Here’s how most demos go.

4
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:15,040
This is the home screen, here’s
the menu, these are the settings.

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:18,880
Every screen is shown, but by the
end, there’s nobody to care about,

6
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:22,400
and nothing to remember. Now, try this.

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,310
Amara arrives in a new city at midnight.

8
00:00:25,310 --> 00:00:30,520
Her flat was empty. No pot,
no lamp, no one to ask.

9
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:33,360
Already, you want to know what happens to her.

10
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,780
That’s the difference a story makes.

11
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:38,320
So, which one would you keep watching?

12
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:44,300
The walkthrough or Amara? Before you
build any slides, write one sentence.

13
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,760
Who your project helps, what changes for them,

14
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,320
and what you want your audience to do.

15
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:54,230
Here’s an example for a made-up
project called Borrow Box.

16
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:57,050
Notice that it’s under 25 words.

17
00:00:57,050 --> 00:01:02,400
Then test it. Read your sentence to a
partner and have them ask, “So what?”

18
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:05,600
Your first answer is usually a feature.

19
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:06,560
Keep going.

20
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:11,280
By the last round, you’re talking about
a person and what changes for them.

21
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:14,400
That’s the answer your demo is really about.

22
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:17,680
Every good demo follows the
same arc in two minutes.

23
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:20,730
Context: one person, one moment.

24
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:23,910
Conflict: why it’s hard for them today.

25
00:01:23,910 --> 00:01:26,730
Climax: your product, solving it.

26
00:01:26,730 --> 00:01:29,530
And closure: what changed, and your ask.

27
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:31,920
And notice where the demo sits.

28
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:34,540
Not everywhere, but right here.

29
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:38,970
As the climax. So, put
one person at the centre.

30
00:01:38,970 --> 00:01:43,600
A persona is fine. We build paper
airplanes, not rocket ships.

31
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:49,280
Give them three things. A name, one
specific moment, and one thing they want.

32
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:52,310
Keep them in every part of your story.

33
00:01:52,310 --> 00:01:56,480
Next, find the one task. What
does your persona come to do?

34
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:59,200
And where does this problem get solved?

35
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:03,440
That screen is your demo. Everything
else can wait for questions.

36
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:06,400
When you show it, don’t narrate your clicks.

37
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:09,390
Not “click the plus icon, then choose a date.”

38
00:02:09,390 --> 00:02:12,080
Instead, say what the person does.

39
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:15,200
“Amara borrows a pot from Joe for Saturday.”

40
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:18,790
And show each screen only when she needs it.

41
00:02:18,790 --> 00:02:22,240
Keep the demo itself to about 45 seconds.

42
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:26,320
The rest of your two minutes
is the story around it.

43
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:31,820
Then, end where you began. With
your person, now better off.

44
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:34,480
Amara cooks her first meal at home.

45
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:38,480
Then, make your ask, clearly and specifically.

46
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:41,680
We’d like one neighbourhood
to try it this month.

47
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:46,710
Now it’s your turn. One, write
your “so what” sentence.

48
00:02:46,710 --> 00:02:49,530
Two, write five beat cards.

49
00:02:49,530 --> 00:02:53,360
Three, cut your demo down to one task.

50
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:58,640
Four, record two versions in different
orders, and keep the stronger one.

51
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:02,350
You’ll find the worksheets
in the handbook, Chapter 1.

52
00:03:02,350 --> 00:03:09,520
That’s Video 1. Next time, how
to make your story believable.
